= The Bezrukavnikov-Finkelberg equivalence

We attempt to give a rather complete sketch of the infamous (at least among my friends) article of Bezrukavnikov-Finkelberg. The main theorem of the article is the following: 

#thm[
	$D^check(G)_perf (U_(planck)^square)tilde.eq D_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr)$, and $D^check(G)_perf (Sym^square (check(frak(g))))tilde.eq D_(L^+G)(Gr)$. 
]

where the algebras $U_(planck)^square$ and $Sym^square (check(frak(g)))$ are to be defined later. The strategy of the proof is given by the following steps. 

+	Construct functors $kappa_planck:cal(H C)_planck->QCoh^(GG_m)(M_((check(frak(t))\/W)^2)Delta)$ and $kappa:Coh^(check(G)times GG_m)(check(frak(g))^*)->Coh^(GG_m)(T(check(frak(t))^*\/W))$. The functors are fully faithful on the subcategory of free objects, namely $cal(H C)_planck^fr$ and $Coh_fr^(check(G)times GG_M)(check(frak(g))^*)$. 
+	Extend the Satake functor $S:Rep(check(G))->Perv_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr)$ to fully faithful functors 
	$ S_planck:cal(H C)_planck^fr->D_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr), #[and] S_(q c):Coh_fr^(check(G)times GG_m)(check(frak(g))^*)->D_(L^+G)(Gr) $
	These functors are constructed via the fully faithful cohomology functors 
	$ H^*_(L^+G times.r GG_m):Perv_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr)->Coh^(GG_m)(M_((check(t)\/w)^2)Delta),H^*_(L^+G):Perv_(L^+G)(Gr)->Coh^(GG_m)(T(check(frak(t))^*\/W)) $
	and by analysing the images, we have $kappa_planck|_(cal(H C)_planck^fr)$ and $kappa|_(Coh^(check(G)times GG_m)_fr (check(frak(g))^*))$ factors through the cohomology functors by $S_planck$ and $S_(q c)$, i.e., $kappa_planck=H^*_(L^+G times.r GG_m)compose S_planck$ and $kappa=H^*_(L^+G)compose S_(q c)$. 
+	Extend $S_planck$ and $S_(q c)$ to our final equivalences via the fully faithful inclusions $cal(H C)_planck^fr subset D^check(G)_perf (U_(planck)^square)$ and $Coh_fr^(check(G)times GG_m)(check(frak(g))^*)subset D^check(G)_perf (Sym^square (check(frak(g))))$. 

We will expand the three steps one by one. 

== Step 1

=== Computations of equivariant cohomology 

Before we start, we must compute the equivariant cohomologies of points. 

#def[
	Let $X$ be a scheme and $Z subset X$ be a closed subscheme classified by the quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals $cal(I)$. We construct the deformation to the normal cone as following. Firstly, take $AA^1=Spec k[planck]$. Then, consider the following extended Rees $cal(O)_X$-algebra $cal(R)$ given by the sheaf of subalgebras in $cal(O)_X [planck^plus.minus]$ generated by $planck$ and $f planck^(-1)$ where $f in cal(I)$. We define the deformation to the normal cone $M_X Z=Spec_X cal(R)$. 
]

#def[
	For groups $G$ with $H^*_G (pt)$ is an integral domain, we define the localized equivariant cohomology $H^*_G (X)_loc=H^*_G (X)times.o_(H^*_G (pt))Frac(H^*_G (pt))$. 
]

#thm[
	There is an equivalence 
	$ alpha:cal(O)(N_((check(frak(t))\/W)^2)Delta)tilde.eq plus.o.big_(pi_1(G))H^*_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr) $ 
	Here $Delta$ denote the diagonal embedding. 
]
#proof[
	We first treat the case where $G$ is simply connected. 

	Firstly, notice $H^*_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr)=H^*_(GG_m)(L^+G\\L G\/L^+G)$ admit a bimodule structure of $H^*_(L^+G)(pt)$. Since we have the homotopy equivalence $L^+G tilde.eq G$ by contractibility of $k[[t]]$, we have $H^*_(L^+G)(pt)=H^*_G (pt)=cal(O)(frak(t)\/W)$ by Chern-Weil theory. Thus, we have $H^*_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr)$ is a $H^*_(L^+G)(pt) times.o H^*_(L^+G)(pt) times.o H^*_(GG_m)(pt)=cal(O)((frak(t)\/W)^2 times AA^1)$. This gives a structure map 
	$ alpha:cal(O)((frak(t)\/W)^2 times AA^1)->H^*_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr) $
	Then we show it extends to the extended Rees algebra $cal(R)$, that is, we show that for $f in cal(I)_Delta$, we have $alpha(f)$ contains a factor of $alpha(planck)$ where we regard $H^*_(GG_m)(pt)=k[planck]$, i.e., the two maps $H_(L^+G)^*(pt)->H^*_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr)\/planck$ coincide. This is because $H^*_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr)\/planck=H^*(L^+G\\L G\/L^+G)$. Thus we obtain a map 
	$ alpha:cal(O)(M_((frak(t)\/W)^2)Delta)->H^*_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr) $ 
	We then show it is an equivalence. For injectivity, note $H^*_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(pt)=cal(O)(frak(t)\/W times AA^1)$. Notice by definition that $cal(O)(M_((frak(t)\/W)^2)Delta)$ is an integral domain, so it suffices to prove the localized morphism 
	$ alpha_loc:cal(O)(M_((frak(t)\/W)^2)Delta)times.o_(cal(O)(frak(t)\/W times AA^1))Frac(cal(O)(frak(t) times AA^1))->H^*_(L^+G times.r GG_m)(Gr)times.o_(cal(O)(frak(t)\/W times AA^1))Frac(cal(O)(frak(t) times AA^1)) $
	is an injection. This is because the right hand side can be identified with the localized equivariant cohomology $H^*_(T times GG_m) (Gr)_loc$. 
]

=== The involved categories 

#def[
	Let $U=U(check(frak(g)))$, and let $U_planck$ denote the graded enveloping algebra, explicitly, the graded $k[planck]$-algebra generated by $check(frak(g))$ under the relations $x y-y x=planck[x,y]$. Equivalently, $U_planck$ is the Rees algebra of the filtered algebra $U$. 
	
	These enveloping algebras are equipped with the structure of $check(G)$-representations via the adjoint action $check(G) lact check(frak(g))$. 
]

#def[
	We define the category of $planck$-Harish-Chandra bimodules $tilde(cal(H C))_planck$ to be the category with the following objects with the requirements: 
	+	An object $M$ is a graded $U_planck times.o_(k[planck])U_planck$-module equipped with the structure of a $check(G)$-representation $rho:check(G) lact M$, such that:
	+	the action map $U_planck times.o_(k[planck])U_planck times.o M->M$ is $check(G)$-equivariant, and, 
	+	for $x in frak(check(g))$, the action of $1 times.o x+x times.o 1$ on $M$ is $planck dot d rho(x)$. 
	The intuition to this definition is some sort of Harish-Chandra modules of Harish-Chandra pairs. 

	We define $cal(H C)_planck subset tilde(cal(H C))_planck$ to be the full subcategory spanned by the modules $M$ such that $M$ is finitely generated as $U_planck times.o 1$-modules. 
]

= Koszul duality

== A first glance: Koszul duality for finite type groups

=== Highest weight categories 

In this section, we introduce a special type of Abelian categories which behaves like the category of perverse sheaves over a suitable stratified space or the BGG category $cal(O)$. The history of the developments of such categories is complicated, resulting in various definitions of such categories (some of which are simple generalizations of others). We will begin with the first definition. 

#def[
	Let $cal(A)$ be an Abelian category over some field $k$. We say $cal(A)$ is highest weight in the sense of @Highestwt1 if the following conditions are met.
	+	$cal(A)$ satisfies the condition $"AB5"$ and is locally Artinian. That is, every object is generated by finite-length objects by filtered colimits. In particular, every object admit a (possibly infinite) composition series. 
	+	There is an interval finite poset $Lambda$ (that is, the intervals $[mu,nu]={lambda|mu<=lambda<=nu}subset Lambda$ are finite) with the set of all non-isomorphic simple objects $IC_lambda in cal(A)$ indexed by $Lambda$. 
	+	There is a collection of costandard objects $nabla_lambda$ indexed by $Lambda$. 
	+	These objects contain $IC_lambda subset nabla_lambda$ as a subobject, and the quotient $nabla_lambda\/IC_lambda$ contains only $IC_mu$ with $mu>lambda$ in the composition series. Moreover, $[nabla_mu:IC_nu]$ and $dim_k Hom(nabla_mu,nabla_nu)$ are finite. 
	+	$IC_lambda$ admit an injective envelope $I_lambda$ in $cal(A)$. This injective $I_lambda$ admit another complete filtration 
		$ 0=F_0 subset F_1 subset dots.c subset I_lambda $
		such that $F_1=nabla_lambda$, and $F_i\/F_(i-1)=nabla_mu$ where $mu>lambda$ for $i>1$. Moreover, each $nabla_mu$ appears only finitely many times. 
] <Def_Highest_wt_cat_def_1>

This is not the main focus of this section, so we only present the main theorem of the article. 

#thm(name: [Theorem 3.9, @Highestwt1])[
	Finite length highest weight categories with finitely many simple objects are always of the form as a right module category $Mod_A^(f.g.)$ for some right quasi-hereditary algebra $A$. 
] <Thm_CPS_highest_wt_heri_mod>

This is mainly used in the paper @KoszulFin. In their paper, they only focused on finite length categories with finitely many simple objects, namely, the category of perverse sheaves with respect to a nice enough finite stratification. Their definitions went as follows. 

#def[
	Let $cal(A)$ be an Abelian category over some field $k$. We say $cal(A)$ is highest weight in the sense of @KoszulFin if the following conditions hold. 
	+	$cal(A)$ is of finite length. 
	+	$cal(A)$ has simple objects indexed by a finite poset $Lambda$, denoted by $IC_lambda$. Moreover, $End(IC_lambda)=k$. The poset $Lambda$ will be referred to as the weight poset. 
	+	There are standard and costandard objects $Delta_lambda$ and $nabla_lambda$ indexed by $Lambda$, equipped with structure morphisms $Delta_lambda->IC_lambda$ and $IC_lambda->nabla_lambda$. 
	+	Let $Lambda' subset Lambda$ be any subposet closed under taking smaller elements. We define $cal(A)_(Lambda')$ to be the subcategory of $cal(A)$ spanned by objects with simple components $IC_(lambda')$ for $lambda' in Lambda'$. Then, for any maximal element $m in Lambda'$, we have $Delta_m->IC_m$ a projective cover and $IC_m->nabla_m$ an injective hull in $cal(A)_(Lambda')$. 
	+	$ker(Delta_lambda->IC_lambda)$ and $coker(IC_lambda->nabla_lambda)$ lie in $cal(A)_(<lambda)$. 
	+	$Ext^2(Delta_mu, nabla_nu)=0$. 
] <Def_Highest_wt_cat_def_2>

#lem[
	We have the following orthogonality and Ext vanishing conditions: 
	$ dim_k Hom(Delta_mu,nabla_nu)=delta_(mu,nu),Ext^i(Delta_mu,nabla_nu)=0 $
] <Lem_orthog_ext_vanish_highest_wt>
#proof[
	For the first orthogonality condition, note $Delta_mu->IC_mu$ is a projective cover in $cal(A)_(<=mu)$, so every quotient of $Delta_mu$ must have $IC_mu$ as a factor. Similarly, every subobject of $nabla_nu$ must intersect $IC_nu$. Thus, if there is a non-trivial map $Delta_mu->nabla_nu$, since the image and coimage is non-zero, we have 
	$ [Delta_mu:IC_nu]!=0, [Delta_nu:IC_mu]!=0 $
	which implies $nu<=mu$ and $mu<=nu$, so $mu=nu$. Then, to compute $dim_k Hom(Delta_mu,nabla_mu)$, by the fifth axiom, we have 
	$ Hom(Delta_mu,nabla_mu)=End(IC_mu)=k $
	For the $Ext^1$ vanishing, we put $Lambda' = {<=mu}union{<=nu}$. Then, either $mu$ or $nu$ or both is maximal in $Lambda'$, so the exact sequence 
	$ 0->nabla_nu->M->Delta_mu->0 $
	splits by either projectivity of $nabla_nu$ or injectivity of $Delta_mu$ in $cal(A)_(Lambda')$. 
	
	For higher $Ext$ vanishing, we prove by descending induction on $mu$. If $mu$ is maximal in $Lambda$, then $Delta_mu$ is projective and there is nothing to prove. In general, consider $P_mu->IC_mu$, which has a standard filtration with $Delta_mu$ the last term. Thus considering the long exact sequence of $Hom(-,nabla_nu)$ applied to 
	$ 0-->K-->P_mu-->Delta_mu-->0 $
	we have
	$ Ext^(i-1)(K,nabla_nu)-->Ext^i (Delta_mu,nabla_nu)-->Ext^i (P_mu,nabla_nu) $
	exact with the first term and last term 0 by induction. Thus the claim follows. 
]

#prop[
	Such categories have enough projective objects, with each projective admitting a finite filtration with standard associated graded pieces. The dual argument applies for injective objects. 
]
#proof[
	We prove the following statement by induction: There are enough projectives in $cal(A)_(Lambda')$, and each one admit a standard filtration. 

	We prove by induction on the cardinality of $Lambda'$. The case where $card Lambda'=1$ is trivial. Then, suppose $Lambda'$ and $Lambda' union {lambda}$ are both closed under taking smaller elements. We will prove the statement for $Lambda' union {lambda}$ from the statement for $Lambda'$. 
	
	Now, for any $mu in Lambda' union {lambda}$, we attempt to construct a projective cover of $IC_mu$ in $cal(A)_(Lambda' union {lambda})$. Since $Lambda'$ is closed under taking smaller elements, it is necessary that $lambda$ is an maximal element. Thus, $Delta_lambda->IC_lambda$ is a desired projective cover. Then, for $mu in Lambda'$, we wish to upgrade the projective cover $P->IC_mu$ in $cal(A)_(Lambda')$ to a projective cover in $cal(A)_(Lambda' union {lambda})$. We put $E=Ext^1(P,Delta_lambda)$. Since $P$ is not yet projective, $E$ somehow measures the extend that $P$ is non-projective. Then consider the unit element in 
	$ 1 in E times.o E^*=Ext^1(P,E^* times.o Delta_lambda) $
	This gives rise to an extension 
	$ 0-->E^* times.o Delta_lambda-->tilde(P)-->P-->0 $
	such that the composition 
	$ E->Hom(E^* times.o Delta_lambda,Delta_lambda)->^delta Ext^1(P,Delta_lambda) $
	is the identity. Then, since the first map is an isomorphism because $End(Delta_lambda)=End(IC_lambda)=k$, the boundary map is also an isomorphism. Then, by projectivity of $Delta_lambda$, $Ext^1(Delta_lambda,Delta_lambda)=0$. Hence, the long exact sequence 
	$ Hom(E^* times.o Delta_lambda,Delta_lambda)-->Ext^1(P,Delta_lambda)-->Ext^1(tilde(P),Delta_lambda)-->Ext^1(E^* times.o Delta_lambda,Delta_lambda) $
	gives $Ext^1(tilde(P),Delta_lambda)=0$. Now, it suffices to prove that $tilde(P)$ is a projective cover for $IC_mu$. This is equivalent to saying 
	$ dim_k Hom(tilde(P),IC_nu)=delta_(mu,nu),Ext^1(tilde(P),IC_nu)=0 $
	where the second condition ensures that $tilde(P)$ is projective and the first condition ensures that $tilde(P)->IC_mu$ is a cover. Consider the exact sequence 
	$ Ext^1(P,IC_nu)-->Ext^1(tilde(P),IC_nu)-->Ext^1(E^* times.o Delta_lambda,IC_nu) $
	the first term vanishes by the projectivity of $P$ in $cal(A)_Lambda'$, and the third vanishes by projectivity of $Delta_lambda$. So the second group vanishes. By the same reason, $dim_k Hom(tilde(P),IC_nu)=delta_(mu,nu)$. Thus, it suffices to prove the statement for $IC_lambda$. Then, to compute $Hom(tilde(P),IC_lambda)$, consider the exact sequence 
	$ 0-->K-->Delta_lambda-->IC_lambda-->0 $
	Then, $Hom(tilde(P),IC_lambda)=0$ because $K$ lies in $cal(A)_(Lambda')$ and run the exact sequence, and $Ext^1$ vanishes by the vanishing condition of $Ext^2$. 

	Hence, the original statement follows from induction. 
]

#coro[
	BGG reciprocity holds, that is, we have $(P_mu:Delta_nu)=[nabla_nu:L_mu]$
]
#proof[
	By the Hom orthogonality, the first number is equal to $dim_k Hom(P_mu,nabla_nu)$. For the second, consider the Jordan-Holder series of $nabla_nu$ 
	$ 0=F_0 subset dots.c subset F_n=nabla_nu $
	By the projectivity of $P_mu$, we have 
	$ 0-->Hom(P_mu,F_(i-1))-->Hom(P_mu,F_i)-->Hom(P_mu,IC_lambda)-->0 $
	exact, where $IC_lambda$ is a simple factor. But then, since $P_mu->IC_mu$ is a projective cover, $P_mu->IC_lambda$ is nontrivial iff $lambda=mu$, so the second number is equal to $dim_k Hom(P_mu,nabla_nu)$. 
]

#prop[
	Let $X=product.co_(lambda in Lambda)X_lambda$ be an affine stratification. Then, $Perv_Lambda (X)$ is a highest weight category in the sense of @KoszulFin. 
] <Prop_Perv_highest_wt>
#proof[
	We verify the axioms. The first three axioms are obtained directly by the properties of perverse sheaves on an affine stratified space. For the fourth property, it suffices to prove that $*$- or $!$-restriction to the open strata is exact (which is standard), because 
	$ Hom(Delta,-)=Hom(j_!1,-)=Hom(1,j^*(-))=j^*(-) $
	and the dual holds for $nabla$. Moreover, these objects are indecomposable, so they are the projective cover and injective hull. The fifth property also follows from the property of intermediate extensions. The final property is slightly more difficult, and is proven by the following property of perverse sheaves: firstly, by an analysis of extensions we have 
	$ Ext^2_Perv_Lambda (Delta_mu,nabla_nu)subset Hom^2_(D_Lambda)(Delta_mu,nabla_nu) $
	and for the latter, we have 
	$ 
	Hom^2_(D_Lambda)(Delta_mu,nabla_nu)=&Hom_D_Lambda (j_(mu!)1_mu [dim X_mu],j_(nu*)1_nu [dim X_nu+2]) \
	=&Hom_D_Lambda (j_nu^*j_(mu!)1_mu [dim X_mu],1_nu [dim X_nu+2])
	$
	Then, the first term is 0 for $nu!=mu$, and if $nu=mu$, then the above Hom is an ext group of vector spaces, which is 0. 
]

#coro[
	We have the realization functor an equivalence. 
	$ D(Perv_Lambda (X))->^tilde.eq D_Lambda (X) $
]
#proof[
	Note that $P_lambda$ and $nabla_lambda$ separately generates both $D(Perv_Lambda (X))$ and $D_Lambda (X)$. It suffices to show that 
	$ Ext^i_(Perv_Lambda (X))(P_mu,nabla_nu)=Hom^i_(D_Lambda (X))(P_mu,nabla_nu) $
	By projectivity of $P_mu$, the left hand side vanishes unless $i=0$. The right side also vanishes unless $i=0$ by the standard filtration of $P$ and applying the property of $!$-pushforwards. The statement for $i=0$ is trivial. 
]

Now, we may wish to generalize the above properties to possibly infinite stratifications. This is where the modern definition of highest weight categories come in. 

#def[
	Let $cal(A)$ be an Abelian category over some field $k$. We say $cal(A)$ is highest weight in the sense of @Highestwt2 if it is highest weight in the sense of @KoszulFin, but with the condition of the finite poset $Lambda$ replaced by for any $lambda in Lambda$, the set ${mu|mu<=lambda}subset Lambda$ is finite. 
] <Def_Highest_wt_cat_def_3>

Properties such as the orthogonality and Ext vanishing still holds. However, the category of perverse sheaves over a stratified space may not be highest weight, for example 
$ D(I\\L G\/I)!=D(Perv(I\\L G\/I)) $
which is an evidence of $Perv(I\\L G\/I)$ not being highest weight. Later on, we will adopt this definition of highest weight categories. 

#lem[
	Let $Lambda' subset Lambda$ be a subposet closed under taking smaller objects, and let $cal(A)$ be a highest weight category with weight poset $Lambda$. Then, the Serre quotient $pi:cal(A)->>cal(A)\/cal(A)_Lambda'$ is a highest weight category with weight poset $Lambda-Lambda'$. Its simple, standard, and costandard objects are the image of the projection $pi$ of the original simple, standard, and costandard objects. 
] <Lem_Serre_quot_highest_wt>
#proof[
	Axioms (1), (2), (4), (5) are obvious. Then, for (4), we claim that we have an isomorphism 
	$ Hom_(cal(A))(Delta_lambda,N)=Hom_(cal(A)\/cal(A)_(Lambda'))(pi(Delta_lambda),pi(N)) $
	for $lambda in Lambda-Lambda'$. This is because a map $pi(Delta_lambda)->pi(N)$ is represented by a map $M'->N\/N'$ in $cal(A)$, where $M' subset Delta_lambda$ and $N' subset N$ such that $Delta_lambda\/M$ and $N'$ is in $cal(A)_Lambda$. Then, since every quotient of $Delta_lambda$ necessarily contains $IC_lambda in.not cal(A)_Lambda$, we have $M'=Delta_lambda$. Then, since $N' in cal(A)_Lambda'subset cal(A)_(<=lambda)$, and that $Delta_lambda$ is projective in $cal(A)_(<=lambda)$, we have $Ext^1(Delta_lambda,N')=0$, which implies that the map $Delta_lambda->N\/N'$ factors through $N->N\/N'$. Hence, we have the above map is surjective. Moreover since $M'=Delta_lambda$ necessarily, and any map $Delta_lambda->N\/N'$ does note annihilate $IC_lambda$, it is non-zero. Thus the above map is an isomorphism. This implies that $Delta_lambda$ is projective in $(cal(A)\/cal(A)_Lambda')_(Lambda'')$ for $lambda$ maixmal in $Lambda''$. 

	Finally for axiom $(6)$, it suffices to prove the case where $Lambda$ is finite becuase to compute $Ext^2(pi(Delta_mu),pi(nabla_nu))$, it suffices to compute this in the category $cal(A)_(<=mu,nu)$, which is finite by assumption. However, in the finite case, we have enough projectives, which allows us to prove the claim as follows. We consider the object $pi(P_mu)->>pi(Delta_mu)$. The kernel of this map has a filtration by $pi(Delta_lambda)$ where $lambda>mu$. Then, if $pi(P_mu)$ is projective, the above vanishing of $Ext^2$ follows by the long exact sequence given by 
	$ Ext^1(K,pi(nabla_nu))-->Ext^2(pi(Delta_mu),pi(nabla_nu))-->Ext^2(pi(P_mu),pi(nabla_nu)) $
	where the last term is 0 by projectivity and the first is 0 by analysing extensions. Thus it suffices to prove projectivity. If not, consider a non-split surjection $pi(M)->>pi(P_mu)$. This surjection is represented by a map $M'->P_mu\/N'$ where $M\/M'$ and $N'$ lies in $cal(A)_(Lambda')$. Moreover, by surjectivity, this map must have cokernel lying in $cal(A)_(Lambda')$. However, since $P_mu->>IC_mu$ is a projective cover with $mu in.not Lambda'$, the cokernel must necessarily vanish, so the representing map is itself surjective. Thus, $M'->>P_mu$ splits, which contradicts $pi(M)->>pi(P_mu)$ not split. 
]

